ross was a fantastic young man the kind  of song that any parent would be  proud of and we were certainly extremely  proud of him and remain  proud of him to this day he  was a devoted family man he  loved his little son charlie who's just  three years old and enjoyed nothing  better than to  play with charlie uh take him swimming  ross was quite  active and sporty and uh he couldn't  uh go to his tennis club or to the gym  uh because of covid uh the last one we  saw him was  christmas we stuck to the raws and that  was the last time we saw him face to  face  we spoke to him virtually every day on  facetime um but ross was  funny he was we had many great laughs  together uh he was a dynamic charismatic  young man who was full of life and  love he loved his family but he was  someone who suffered terribly with  severe depression  and he battled for more than 10 years  to fight that depression and he fought  very hard and tried  most things to try and overcome it and  reach salvation um ross thought that he  had  cracked it to use his praise and he had  to  find and pay for his own counselor and  he particularly  related to his last counsellor and  thought that he turned the corner and  then  uh the depression came back with a  vengeance and  we'll never know for sure but we think  that that's what  um finally pulled the rug from under  russell's feet and he took his life on  the 21st of february  on paper he had so much to live for  a beautiful little boy that we've seen  in your video footage  a fiance a loving family  a great life how much of a factor do you  think  lockdown was in that final decision to  take his life  lockdown and the restrictions associated  with it  didn't kill ross uh it wasn't that that  took his life but it  certainly added to the weight of the  burden that he was carrying  he was due to marry his fiance charlotte  last year  that had to be postponed because  of lockdown her  wedding dress is buried alongside ross  um they were waiting to get married um  when the restrictions were starting to  be  eased and along with all those sort of  restrictions that i mentioned  earlier um you know to do with his sort  of active sporting life and to do with  his family  uh in particular um played quite a sort  of  quite a a hand in what happened  uh to ross but he was also very  frustrated  with the mental health services  we know that there are many  fantastically dedicated people working  out there within the  mental health services but ross was  frustrated  that uh having tried so hard and tried  so many things for ten years that he was  then put on a waiting list for six  months and he died  while he was on that waiting list for  therapy  and my view is and what i'm trying to  to get across now is that you know ross  had what i regard as cancer of the mind  he was in effect terminally ill and if  someone turns up at a doctor's doors or  at a hospital's doors with terminal  cancer  they aren't turned away for six months  and told to  come back after that period um  the mental health provision in this  country i am learning since we lost  ross is woefully inadequate when it  comes to  crisis care and when somebody has taken  the very brave  um step of of contacting  the authorities to say that that is how  they feel  there isn't really the help for them  i've been talking to a lot of people on  social media and  and talking about uh my family's story  and about ross in particular and i've  been inundated with the  messages from people who are in a  situation like  me and my family who  also say that their son their daughter  their father their mother their cousin  whatever  they also died while on a waiting list  and i think  that there has to be some kind of parity  with physical  illness and we have to take these  statistics  very very seriously much more seriously  than we do  at the moment in a few years god willing  covid will be a distant memory  but the suicide rate will still amount  to a pandemic  year in year out every year around the  globe  800 000 people around the world every  year  it's the biggest killer of men under the  age of 45  in this country not cancer not drugs not  road accidents  suicide young men are the biggest  threat to their own futures and their  own lives i was always shocked by that  statistic  even before ross died and the fact that  75  of people who take their own lives are  men is something that we have to look  far more seriously at every suicide  is an equal tragedy be it a man or a  woman  but there is a particular problem with  men and i think it's a cultural societal  problem that we also need to address  um ross wrote a farewell letter to  members of his family am i right in  thinking mike  that he wanted you to do this now to  carry on this work to raise awareness  of mental health issues and the lack of  support that he was able to access in  those  final months he did it was uh quite a  long letter and  uh ross was particularly fond of a  charity called calm  c-a-l-m campaign against living  miserably and he asked  his family to go on campaigning he  addressed each one of us  in turn and he  um said to his son charlie um  he'll be four next month that he thought  he could possibly live on  through charlie without the faults  we of course as parents didn't think  that ross had any faults  um but yeah that was one of the things  and his brother and sister tom and  laura and his mom glennis we've all been  involved in the campaign to try and  raise awareness to try and change the  language around  uh men's health and men's mental health  in particular  to try and get rid of the stigma uh for  example you know we talk about  manning up when men are going through  difficult times  there's a phrase strong silent type for  example  and all of these things i think mean  shut up  don't express it take it on the chin if  you're vulnerable  you're weak and if there's anything that  i would like to get across in russia's  name and in ross's memory  it's that message that to all  all people who are suicidal but young  men in particular  if you are vulnerable and you ask for  help  that is a strength if you cry that is  not  a weakness we men in particular have to  be  more open about this and if we can't get  the language right and we can't get rid  of the stigma  what progress will we ever make and mike  in your experience  in the months following ross's death  how comfortable do you think people are  talking to you  about it um in most cases people have  been exceptionally kind and caring we  have great  um we're very close to our family our  immediate family has always been  very close we did speak to ross a lot  about his  uh problems with with mental health um  and we're supporting each other we're  clinging to each other  we get a great deal of help from the  wider family  and friends and neighbors and people  from all over the world have made  contact with me to  express their condolences and i can't  thank them enough from the bottom of my  heart  and i always just always used to think  as a sky news correspondent  when we had to speak to people who were  bereaved for some reason i always used  to worry about what i could say or do  and came to the conclusion i guess that  there was nothing that i could really  say or do to ease their pain but  since we lost ross i've realized that  actually  um knowing that people are there they've  got your back and they're ready to sort  of catch you if you're  if you fall does make a difference and  what i would say to people is i  understand if you feel  uncomfortable talking about suicide it's  a highly highly sensitive  subject and different people deal with  it in different ways i can only speak to  for myself and my family and i think  mostly we feel that we would rather  speak about ross and  talk about the happy memories that we've  got  because even if we wanted to we'd never  forget him because he was that kind of  person we don't want to forget him he  made the wrong decision and i think he  would ultimately  have found salvation suicide is never  the  answer but that said we'll always be so  proud  to have had him that's our son  mike thank you so much for sharing  ross's story  and talking to us today mike mccarthy  there
 

